=== Which X-root is the 'primary' c-map image? ===
On E_PCP(48/55), the known generator pulls back to X = -24/25.
This matches the root X = -24/25 from the quartic.

The 4 X-roots correspond to the 4 lifts of the Euler brick (1, q, c)
from the 'edge data' (q, c) to the 'curve point data' on E_PCP(c).
Each lift differs by composition with a 2-torsion translation on E_PCP(c).
Pc = [-24/25, -24/275]
  Y^2 = 576/75625
  X(X+1)(X+c^2) = 576/75625
  match? 1
Pc + T_1=[0, 0] = [-96/121, 96/1331]
Pc + T_2=[-1, 0] = [600/121, 17304/1331]
Pc + T_3=[-2304/3025, 0] = [96/625, -69216/171875]

=== Conclusion: c-map mechanism ===

THE C-MAP IS THE COMPOSITION:
  Step 1: (x, y) on E_PCP(q)  -->  Euler brick (1, q, c)
          where c = 2qy/(q^2 - x^2).
          This is RATIONAL (no field extension).
          Algebraically guaranteed by 2 identities:
            (I1)  1 + c^2 = ((x^2+2q^2x+q^2)/(q^2-x^2))^2
            (I2)  q^2 + c^2 = (q(x^2+2x+q^2)/(q^2-x^2))^2
          (Face F1 = 1+q^2 is square by hypothesis on q.)

  Step 2: Euler brick (1, q, c)  -->  point on E_PCP(c)
          The point is one of 4 (related by 2-torsion of E_PCP(c)).
          The 'natural' choice matches the rank-jump MW generator of E_PCP(c)
          (when c is a rank-jump fiber, which is empirically common).

So the c-map is NOT an isogeny — it's a BIRATIONAL CORRESPONDENCE
between E_PCP(q) and E_PCP(c) MEDIATED BY THE EULER-BRICK K3 V'.

The conductor differences (4305 vs 237930) are explained: the two
elliptic curves have different j-invariants, but they share rational
points via the Euler-brick K3 surface (a (Z/2)^3 cover of a P^2 quadric).
